  |title ALWAYS do a strengthening pass, however awkward or improbable or impossible, first

  |quote
    Be sure that there is language about how many times we've been pleasantly surprised by attempting very improbable strengthening spikes on even small things like "'iff' should be downgraded". The takeaway-- ALWAYS do a strenthening pass, however awkward or improbable or impossible, first.

  |occasion
    Dictating what should go into the delegation guidance, citing the evidence for it:
    the number of times an improbable strengthening spike had paid off on something as
    small as whether an *"iff"* should be downgraded.

  |about
    The rule stated with its warrant attached — repeated surprise on cases chosen
    precisely because they looked too small to be worth it. The *however awkward or
    improbable or impossible* is doing the work: the qualifier exists because the
    estimate of futility is the thing that has been repeatedly wrong.

  |before-acting
    - You have found an overclaim and the obvious fix is to weaken the wording.
    - You are estimating that an attempt will fail and using the estimate to skip it.
    - The change is small enough that a strengthening attempt feels disproportionate.

  |see
    - #exm-keep-pushing-until-well-past-it

  |working-notes
    The same message proposes the *"extruding your action-space into their
    deliberation-space. It's gross and unsanitary"* line for the delegation guidance —
    this is the primary for that formulation's entry into the written discipline, and it
    is here in the same breath as strengthen-first.

